    "content": [
     {
      "type": "text",
      "text": "Localized impetigo: a topical antistaphylococcal antibiotic for 5 to 7 days; mupirocin is the most used in the US, and topical treatment is as effective as or more effective than oral"
     }
    ]
   },
   {
    "type": "paragraph",
    "attrs": {
     "indLeft": 18,
     "indRight": 0,
     "indFirst": -18,
     "spaceBefore": 0,
     "spaceAfter": 0,
     "line": null,
     "align": "left",
     "shade": null,
     "borders": null,
     "marker": {
      "text": "•",
      "font": null,
      "marks": [],
      "tabPt": 18
     }
    },
    "content": [
     {
      "type": "text",
      "text": "Mupirocin 2% ointment is recommended for initial treatment of mild nonbullous and bullous impetigo (SORT A)"
     }
    ]
   },
   {
    "type": "paragraph",
    "attrs": {
     "indLeft": 18,
     "indRight": 0,
     "indFirst": -18,
     "spaceBefore": 0,
     "spaceAfter": 0,
     "line": null,
     "align": "left",
     "shade": null,
     "borders": null,
     "marker": {
      "text": "•",
      "font": null,
      "marks": [],
      "tabPt": 18
     }
    },
    "content": [
     {
      "type": "text",
      "text": "Nasal mupirocin for S. aureus nasal carriers, a potential source of recurrent impetiginization. Decolonization significantly lowers S. aureus infection, but whether it lowers recurrent impetigo specifically is less clear"
     }
    ]
   }
  ]
 },
